

🔷 Block Registry Integration Class

Takeaway: This class is the bridge between the parsed .cmb scenario and the internal tensor/kernels that drive the simulation. It is the “activation layer” of the encrypted engine.

🔶 1. Purpose of the Integration Class

The integration class performs four critical tasks:

Converts BlockToken objects into Block instances

Allocates anonymous tensors for each block

Generates KernelSpec objects for the integrator

Produces a unified ExecutionContext for the simulation

This is the first moment where the engine becomes alive.

🔶 2. High‑Level Workflow

Receive a parsed Scenario

For each token, resolve the block via the registry

Instantiate the block with the active ProjectionProfile

Allocate tensors (anonymous handles)

Collect kernels into a master DAG

Return a ResolvedScenario object

🔶 3. Integration Class (Pseudo‑Code)

class BlockIntegrator:
    def __init__(self, registry, tensor_factory, kernel_graph):
        self.registry = registry
        self.tensor_factory = tensor_factory
        self.kernel_graph = kernel_graph

    def integrate(self, scenario):
        context = self._build_context(scenario)

        resolved_blocks = []
        for token in scenario.block_tokens:
            block_def = self.registry.resolve(token.name)
            block_instance = block_def.build_fn(context)

            # Allocate tensors
            tensors = self._allocate_tensors(block_instance, context)

            # Register kernels
            self._register_kernels(block_instance, tensors)

            resolved_blocks.append(block_instance)

        return ResolvedScenario(
            scenario=scenario,
            blocks=resolved_blocks,
            context=context,
            kernel_graph=self.kernel_graph
        )

    def _build_context(self, scenario):
        return ExecutionContext(
            profile=scenario.profile,
            constants=scenario.constants,
            grid_size=(128, 128, 128),
            dt=0.01
        )

    def _allocate_tensors(self, block_instance, context):
        tensors = {}
        for t_spec in block_instance["tensors"]:
            tensors[t_spec["id"]] = self.tensor_factory.create(
                shape=t_spec["shape"],
                dtype="float64"
            )
        return tensors

    def _register_kernels(self, block_instance, tensors):
        for k in block_instance["kernels"]:
            self.kernel_graph.add_kernel(
                op_code=k.op_code,
                inputs=[tensors[i] for i in k.inputs],
                outputs=[tensors[o] for o in k.outputs],
                params=k.params
            )

This class:

Never sees physics

Never interprets meaning

Only manipulates tokens, tensors, and kernels

Exactly the zero‑leakage design you intended.

